[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Modules, Vendoring, and Dependency Management in Go"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"Go modules are the standard dependency management system introduced in Go 1.11. They streamline project structures, eliminate the rigid requirement of placing code inside the GOPATH, and provide reproducible builds through the go.mod and go.sum files."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"1. Initializing a New Module"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"To start a new project with Go modules, create a directory and initialize the module using the 'go mod init' command followed by your module path (usually your repository URL)."},{"order":5,"type":"code","text":"mkdir myapp
cd myapp
go mod init github.com/username/myapp"},{"order":6,"type":"paragraph","text":"This command creates a 'go.mod' file in your root directory. The file tracks your module's path and the Go version, and will eventually list all external dependencies."},{"order":7,"type":"subheading","text":"2. Adding and Managing Dependencies"},{"order":8,"type":"paragraph","text":"When you import third-party packages in your code and run commands like 'go build' or 'go test', Go automatically downloads the required packages and adds them to your 'go.mod' file along with a 'go.sum' file containing cryptographic hashes to verify dependency integrity."},{"order":9,"type":"code","text":"// main.go
package main

import (
    "fmt"
    "github.com/google/uuid"
)

func main() {
    id := uuid.New()
    fmt.Println("Generated UUID:", id.String())
}"},{"order":10,"type":"paragraph","text":"After writing the code above, run the tidy command to automatically clean up unused dependencies and fetch missing ones."},{"order":11,"type":"code","text":"go mod tidy"},{"order":12,"type":"subheading","text":"3. Understanding Vendoring"},{"order":13,"type":"paragraph","text":"Vendoring copies all the dependencies required for your project into a local folder named 'vendor' at the root of your module. This is useful for offline builds, restricted enterprise environments, or strict CI/CD pipelines that do not allow outbound internet connections during the build phase."},{"order":14,"type":"code","text":"go mod vendor"},{"order":15,"type":"bullet_point","text":"The 'go mod vendor' command creates a 'vendor' directory containing your dependencies and a 'modules.txt' metadata file."},{"order":16,"type":"bullet_point","text":"To build your project using the vendored dependencies instead of the global module cache, use the -mod=vendor flag."},{"order":17,"type":"code","text":"go build -mod=vendor"},{"order":18,"type":"subheading","text":"Summary and Best Practices"},{"order":19,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Always commit both 'go.mod' and 'go.sum' to version control."},{"order":20,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Do not commit the 'vendor' folder unless your deployment strategy specifically requires offline dependency storage."},{"order":21,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Regularly run 'go mod tidy' to keep your dependency tree clean and updated."}]
